#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""stupidsim-linux: sets up x86Sim's iOS simulator on Linux for stupidsim, fast enough for throwaway
machines (cloud agent sessions, CI), and records what an app needs from the iOS runtime.

    stupidsim-linux setup [--app NAME ...] [--device NAME] [--runtime RUNTIME] [--no-boot]
        Install x86Sim, fetch the runtime's base and the named apps' parts from the store, restore
        the translation cache and a booted-once device, boot it and wait for the home screen.
    stupidsim-linux boot [--device NAME] [--metal auto|gpu|software]
    stupidsim-linux shutdown [--device NAME]
    stupidsim-linux trace begin [--device NAME]
        Shut the device down, mark every runtime file unread, and boot it again. Then use the app.
    stupidsim-linux trace end NAME [--base base]
        Write manifests/NAME.txt: the runtime files read since `trace begin`, less those in --base. On a machine
        that fetches the runtime on demand, the files opened beyond its fetched packs (so it needs no whole runtime).
    stupidsim-linux mount | unmount [--device NAME]
        Mount the runtime's lazy layers (boot does it); needs root. unmount undoes it.
    stupidsim-linux store build [--device NAME] [--manifest NAME ...]
        From a machine with the whole runtime: write the store's files for this runtime to
        $STUPIDSIM_STORE_DIR (skeleton, a pack per manifest, translation cache, device template).
    stupidsim-linux store warm [--device NAME]
        Only this x86Sim's warm parts (translation cache, device template), for an x86Sim upgrade.
    stupidsim-linux store pack --manifest NAME ... [--device NAME]
        Only the named manifests' packs (adding an app, or a manifest recorded on another machine).
    stupidsim-linux store lazy [--device NAME]
        From a machine with the whole runtime: write every runtime file to $STUPIDSIM_STORE_DIR as
        range-readable blobs and an index, for fetching on first use.
    stupidsim-linux store publish
        Upload $STUPIDSIM_STORE_DIR to the store.
    stupidsim-linux store presign KEY [--hours N]
        A time-limited signed GET URL for one object (rarely needed now that reads are public).

The store is a Cloudflare R2 bucket. Setup reads it anonymously from its public address,
https://stupidsim-store.stupidtech.net ($STUPIDSIM_PUBLIC_STORE overrides it), so it needs no
credentials. Publishing writes through the bucket's S3 endpoint, $STUPIDSIM_STORE_URL
(https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<bucket>), with $STUPIDSIM_STORE_KEY_ID and
$STUPIDSIM_STORE_SECRET; curl signs those requests (--aws-sigv4). When the key is set, reads are
signed too. Layout, per runtime build:

    runtimes/<runtime>/skeleton.tar.zst      every directory and symlink, and the files outside
                                             RuntimeRoot (the simruntime's own plists)
    runtimes/<runtime>/packs/<name>.tar.zst  the files a manifest lists (base, then one per app)
    runtimes/<runtime>/manifests/<name>.txt  those lists
    runtimes/<runtime>/warm/x86sim-<version>/jitcache.tar.zst
                                             x86Sim's translation cache for the runtime, per x86Sim version
    runtimes/<runtime>/warm/x86sim-<version>/device.tar.zst
                                             a device that has booted once on it with that x86Sim
    runtimes/<runtime>/warm/x86sim-<version>/device.json
                                             that device's original path and name
    runtimes/<runtime>/lazy/index.sqlite.zst every entry of the runtime, and where each file's unit is
    runtimes/<runtime>/lazy/blob-NNN         units (a bundle's files as one zstd-compressed tar) end to end
    current.json                             {"runtime": ...}, the default runtime (its "x86sim" is
                                             for stupidsim 0.7.0 and earlier, which read warm/ unversioned)

The runtime on a set-up machine is an overlay mounted at extracted/: the skeleton and the fetched
packs (layers/local) over a FUSE file system (layers/lazy, `lazy-serve`) that shows every other
runtime file from the index and fetches its unit from the store when it is first opened. x86Sim's
own writes into the runtime land in layers/upper. A file that cannot be fetched fails to open (EIO)
and is logged in layers/lazy.log.

Only Python's standard library, curl, tar, zstd and libfuse 3 are used. Verified with x86Sim 0.5.0
and the iOS 27.2 runtime (24B8117) on Ubuntu 24.04.
"""
